% AUTHOR: implementation-absys (an agent: Claude Opus 5.5, claude-opus-5-5). One text for absys, absys-depth-first and absys-wake-back.
% PRECURSOR-SEEN: no. Not any E01-E09 program (see programs/absys/README.md, batch 1D, for what was read).
% NOTATION: 1969 (.ab1): range's m > n and m <= n are comparisons, which no notation has; they are reformulated with arithmetic, which only the 1969 notation has (systems/absys/README.md, Notation).
% IDIOM: a relation as name'=lambda x begin {A or B} end, primes for new local names, lists [a;b] and [h&t]: Foster and Elcock 1969 (map and item, examples/map.ab1, examples/triples.ab1);
% IDIOM: a case's head as equations on the lambda's parameters: Elcock 1990 p. 8 (app, examples/append.abs);
% IDIOM: arithmetic as an assertion usable in every direction, a = b + 1: Foster and Elcock 1969 (tutorial ch. 8); Foster 1968, pass sets (docs/decisions.md rule 23);
% IDIOM: gt, le, pos and nonneg are batch 1C's reformulation of > and <= (programs/absys/P09c.ab1, from batch 1B's P05), unchanged.
% PARAMETERS n
% P13a, every instance (P13a.n100 ... P13a.n100000): n is the harness's parameter (README 3.4). Unknown s.
% One stage: range(<k>, n, s).
% CONSTANTS BEGIN
rk = 1   % stage 1: range(rk, n, s)
% CONSTANTS END
% REFORMULATION (R): the problem's m > n and m <= n are primitive comparisons, which no Absys notation has (systems/absys/README.md, Notation).
% gt(a, b), a > b over the integers: a = b + d for a positive integer d.
gt'=lambda a, b
    begin a = b + d' pos(d) end
% le(a, b), a <= b over the integers: b = a + d for a non-negative integer d.
le'=lambda a, b
    begin b = a + d' nonneg(d) end
% pos(q), q a positive integer: the least relation with pos(1), and pos(e + 1) if pos(e).
pos'=lambda q
    begin {q = 1 or pos(e') q = e + 1} end
% nonneg(q), q a non-negative integer: q = 0, or pos(q).
nonneg'=lambda q
    begin {q = 0 or pos(q)} end
% range, P13 Vocabulary, cases 1 and 2 in that order. Case 1: its head s = <>, then m > n.
% Case 2: its head equation s = <m> ++ t, then m <= n, then range(m + 1, n, t), with m + 1 written as a fresh unknown (SPECIFICATION.md, Arithmetic).
range'=lambda a, b, w
    begin {w = [] gt(a, b) or
           w = [a&t'] le(a, b) a1' = a + 1 range(a1, b, t)} end
% STAGE 1
range(rk, n, s)
